<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Moon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhu is an island in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, between Saaremaa and the mainland, and the third largest in the country. Its people resisted the German crusaders until 1227, when the island was taken in the conquest of Saaremaa. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was held by the Livonian Order. Its villages preserve traditional thatched farmhouses and a distinctive folk dress.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhu.jpg" alt="Muhu" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hiiumaamudeliklubi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koguva_sadam_2015.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Muhu is an island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> in the West Estonian archipelago of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>. It has an area of 208 square kilometres and is the third largest island of Estonia, after Saaremaa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiiumaa">Hiiumaa</a>. The Big Strait divides it from the mainland and the Little Strait from Saaremaa. The German and Swedish name for the island is Moon, and the waters to its north are called the Sea of Straits, or Moon Sound. With four neighbouring islets, Muhu forms a rural municipality in Saare County, whose main settlements are Kuivastu, Liiva and Koguva. Muhu is linked to Saaremaa by the Väinatamm causeway, opened in 1896.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In January 1227 an army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Letts</a>, Germans of Riga and native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livonians</a>, 20,000 men commanded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-modena">William of Modena</a>, crossed on sea ice from the mainland and attacked Saaremaa, the last holdout of the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a>. The Estonians surrendered on Muhu at a circular stronghold called Mona, whose earthworks are still preserved near the causeway between the islands. This ended the Estonian Crusade.</p><p>The Battle of Karuse, also called the Battle on the Ice, was fought on 16 February 1270 on the frozen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> between Muhu and the mainland. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> won a decisive victory over the Livonian Order, in what was the fifth-largest defeat of the Livonian or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Orders</a> in the 13th century. The same waters saw the Battle of Moon Sound between Russian and German naval forces in September and October 1917, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The island has a medieval Lutheran church, St. Catherine's, and the traditional village of Koguva, where the Muhu Museum stands. Muhu is known for having the only traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windmill">windmills</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> that are still operational and for its traditional knitted stockings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Battle of Karuse, Lithuanian forces decisively defeated the Livonian Order on the frozen sea between the island of <b>Muhu</b> and the mainland.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-16">16 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its lands were shared with the Livonian Order, which took parts of Saaremaa and the island of <b>Muhu</b>, while the bishop held most of Läänemaa and western Saaremaa.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-osel-wiek">Bishopric of Ösel-Wiek</a></li></ul></section>
